{"task": "M1", "type": "MIXED", "stability": "live", "arm": "baseline", "repeat": 0, "started": "2026-10-11T01:15:54-04:00", "answer_seconds": 697.6, "wall_seconds": 697.6, "time_to_first_evidence": 77.847, "time_to_first_page_evidence": 77.847, "llm_calls": 75, "llm_calls_by_kind": {"route": 5, "plan": 1, "act": 36, "deep_step": 4, "extract": 13, "answer": 9, "review": 5, "synthesize": 2}, "llm_failed_calls": 1, "llm_seconds": 690.63, "llm_queue_wait_seconds": 1112.92, "prompt_tokens": 53244, "output_tokens": 7979, "llm_prompt_eval_seconds": 236.54, "llm_generation_seconds": 402.65, "decision_calls": 0, "decision_seconds": 0, "decision_prompt_tokens": null, "decisions": {"route:generative": 5, "deep_step:generative": 4, "review:generative": 5}, "searches": 23, "fetches": 8, "web_calls": 31, "fetch_failures": 0, "search_failures": 0, "web_seconds": 49.38, "web_from_cassette": 0, "nodes": 11, "nodes_by_mode": {"WIDE": 1, "ATOM": 9, "DEEP": 1}, "nodes_by_status": {"ok": 11}, "max_depth": 2, "findings": 36, "findings_from_pages": 36, "findings_rejected": 0, "sources": 8, "redundancy": {"objectives_dropped_as_duplicate": 0, "near_duplicate_objective_pairs": 1, "repeated_queries": 0, "urls_read_by_more_than_one_agent": 4, "pages_read_with_no_accepted_finding": 0, "nodes_with_no_evidence": 0, "nodes_cancelled": 0}, "interventions": 3, "budget_hits": ["wrap_up"], "status": "ok", "confidence": "medium", "answer": "NVIDIA's data-center revenue growth over the next 12 months will likely be driven by sustained high pricing due to supply constraints and a maturing secondary market, while facing significant competitive pressure from custom silicon and AMD. Official GPU prices for H100, H200, and B200 units remain elevated between $25,000 and $45,000, with rack-level systems like the GB300 NVL72 priced at $3.7M–$4M [S1]. However, specific discounting strategies or percentages for Q3-Q4 2026 could not be established; only on-demand hourly rates and purchase bands were reported [S1].\n\nRevenue growth is supported by strong enterprise AI adoption trends, with global generative AI spending reaching $644 billion in 2025 and the average number of production models per enterprise rising to 4.2 [S3]. Conversely, NVIDIA's market share has declined from 87% in 2024 to 70–75% in 2026 due to faster growth in custom silicon (15–20% market share) and contested AMD figures [S4][S6][S8]. Supply chain constraints, including extended lead times for power semiconductors and pressure on advanced packaging, continue to impact availability [S2].\n\nUncertainty:\nNo direct evidence links secondary market volumes ($121B) or specific discounting strategies to official revenue projections. Additionally, precise enterprise adoption rates specifically for NVIDIA chips were not found, only general model counts. The exact dollar amount of revenue lost due to supply constraints or competitive pressure could not be quantified.", "audit": {"citations": 6, "unknown_citations": [], "cited_pages": 6, "cited_snippets": 0, "cited_domains": ["alatirok.com", "axis-intelligence.com", "content-site-lovat.vercel.app", "gpusmith.com", "randtech.com", "siliconanalysts.com"], "numbers": 17, "numbers_in_cited_source": 15, "numbers_elsewhere_in_evidence": 2, "numbers_ungrounded": 0, "ungrounded_examples": [], "sentences_with_figures_but_no_citation": 2, "numeric_grounding": 0.882}, "run_dir": "experiments/results/baseline_v1/runs/20261011-012731_baseline_what-are-the-main-factors-that-could-affect-nvid", "answered": true, "coverage_missed": ["export controls / China / trade policy", "company guidance or product roadmap"], "coverage": 0.6, "grounding": 1.0, "quality": 0.8, "cites_authoritative_source": false, "ref_model_seconds": 496.6, "observed_gen_tps": 19.8}
